--TEST--
A nullable value passed where `null` is not allowed, and that would fit without its `null`, is told to write `?? []`, `?? ''` or `?? 0` for a type with an empty value, and to test it with `!= null` for any other type — `rule:types/narrowing`
--FILE--
<?nvs
final class Item
{
}

final class Probe
{
    // `?array<int>` without `null` fits, and `[]` is its empty value.
    public static function entries(?array<int> $a): uint
    {
        return Core\Arr::count($a);
    }

    // The same for a string and for a whole number.
    public static function label(?string $s, ?int $n): void
    {
        string $text = $s;
        int $number = $n;
    }

    // A class has no empty value, so the help names the `!= null` test.
    public static function keep(?Item $item): void
    {
        Item $kept = $item;
    }

    // `?string` would not fit `int` even without its `null`, so there is no help.
    public static function wrong(?string $s): void
    {
        int $number = $s;
    }
}
